  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on simulated agent behaviors derived from survey data rather than observed real-world responses to the implemented policies.",
      "what": "An agent-based model was developed using the Irish National Household Travel Survey to simulate how Dublin commuters shift from private cars to sustainable transport under different policy scenarios. The simulation evaluated soft policies, such as cycling campaigns and travel feedback programs, alongside hard policies like increased motor taxes and fuel prices, measuring changes in travel mode share, behavioral stage progression, and carbon dioxide emissions.",
      "why": "The model demonstrates that combining soft promotional campaigns with financial disincentives yields the greatest reduction in car use and emissions, while showing that tracking psychological stages of behavior change provides a more detailed assessment of policy effectiveness than mode share alone."
    }
